        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Who Should Buy It, and Who Should Skip It?</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>Buy the F63 if</strong> you mostly walk, incline-walk or jog
          a few times a week, want 15% incline on a folding frame, and want
          lifetime frame and motor cover with nothing to subscribe to. It is
          also a sensible pick for a household with mixed fitness levels,
          where one person walks and another jogs.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Skip it if</strong> you're training for a race, running most
          days of the week, or consistently pushing past a moderate jogging
          pace: the smaller motor and 20-inch width will start to feel
          limiting, and a Sole with a 22-inch deck such as the
          <a href="/sole-f85-treadmill/">F85</a> is a better long-term fit.
          If interactive touchscreen coaching matters to you, this plain-console
          machine will disappoint regardless of price — look at Sole's own F80
          or a NordicTrack model instead. And if your household includes anyone
          over the 325-lb weight capacity, look at a model rated higher, such as
          the F80 at 350 lbs, for safety and long-term durability reasons.
        </p>

        <p>
          It's also worth a word for anyone shopping specifically for a
          second or backup <a href="/best-treadmills-for-garage/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>treadmill for a garage</a> gym, guest room, or
          vacation property — the F63's combination of real Sole build
          quality and a genuinely low price makes it a sensible pick for
          secondary use cases where a bigger motor's extra headroom simply
          isn't necessary, and where frame quality matters more than features.
        </p>
        </div>

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Is the Sole F63 Worth It?</h2>
        <p>
          Yes, for the buyer it is built for. The Sole F63 succeeds because it doesn't pretend to be something
          it isn't. It's an honest, well-built entry point into a respected
          treadmill brand, delivering genuine frame quality, real cushioning
          technology, and a lifetime frame and motor warranty most budget competitors can't
          match — all without a subscription requirement. Where it falls
          short is exactly where you'd expect from an entry-level model: the
          motor and deck are sized for walking and light jogging, not
          serious daily running, and stretching it past that use case is
          where owners run into trouble.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you're shopping with a clear-eyed sense of how you'll actually
          use a treadmill — mostly walking, occasional light jogging, a
          budget that tops out under $1,200 — the F63 is one of the more
          honest values in this price bracket. If you already know you'll
          be running regularly, save yourself a future upgrade and start
          with a Sole built for it, such as the F85.
        </p>
